steps.text:
If not lactate, what? In isolated mammalian muscle at body temperature, acidosis itself has little direct effect on force. A stronger candidate is [inorganic phosphate](g:inorganic-phosphate) (Pi), which builds up as PCr is broken down [[c:pi-major]].

A major review lists other players too [[c:fatigue-mechanisms]]:

- changes in ions that weaken the electrical signal along the fibre;
- failing Ca²⁺ release from its store inside the fibre;
- reactive oxygen species.

The old lactate-and-H⁺ explanation is probably of limited importance. Which factor dominates depends on the task, and the brain plays a part as well: the module *Fatigue: peripheral and central* takes this further.
